            <ArticleTitle>Intestinal obstruction from midgut volvulus after laparoscopic appendectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>215</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 30-year-old man who developed a small bowel obstruction from an acute midgut volvulus 8 days after undergoing a laparoscopic appendectomy. There was no evidence of congenital malrotation or midgut volvulus on the initial computed tomography (CT) scan or at laparoscopy. Subsequently, a midgut volvulus developed in the absence of congenital malrotation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
